import{_ as t,o as a,c as r,ao as i}from"./chunks/framework.BMR1_14U.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"The WrapIt driver (experimental)","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"implementation/wrapit.md","filePath":"implementation/wrapit.md","lastUpdated":null}'),s={name:"implementation/wrapit.md"};function o(n,e,d,l,p,c){return a(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i(`<h1 id="The-WrapIt-driver-experimental" tabindex="-1">The WrapIt driver (experimental) <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-WrapIt-driver-experimental" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The WrapIt driver (experimental) {#The-WrapIt-driver-experimental}&quot;">​</a></h1><p><code>gen/wrapit/</code> contains an experimental pipeline that generates the C++ binding module from VTK headers using <a href="https://github.com/grasph/wrapit" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">WrapIt</a> (libclang → CxxWrap code), replacing hand-writing of the bulk method surface.</p><div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Experimental The shipped <code>VTK.jl</code> module still uses the hand-written <code>wrapper/src/vtk_julia.cpp</code>. The WrapIt driver is the automation spike&#39;s deliverable and CI gate; see the <a href="https://github.com/ffrancesco94/VTK.jl/blob/main/docs/wrapit-spike.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">full spike report</a> for the verdict and the four upstream bugs found. :::</p><h2 id="Quick-start" tabindex="-1">Quick start <a class="header-anchor" href="#Quick-start" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Quick start {#Quick-start}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="language-shell vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">shell</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --project=gen/wrapit</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -e</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">   # once</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --project=gen/wrapit</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> gen/wrapit/build.jl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">               # generate + compile</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --project=gen/wrapit</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> gen/wrapit/conformance.jl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">         # analytic-oracle gate</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>build.jl</code> renders the WrapIt config with runtime artifact paths, runs the auto-veto loop, post-processes the generated C++, and compiles the module plus a small shim into <code>gen/wrapit/build/</code> (gitignored).</p><h2 id="Why-WrapIt--shim,-not-a-custom-generator" tabindex="-1">Why WrapIt (+ shim), not a custom generator <a class="header-anchor" href="#Why-WrapIt--shim,-not-a-custom-generator" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why WrapIt (+ shim), not a custom generator {#Why-WrapIt--shim,-not-a-custom-generator}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The spike (2026-10-24) compared WrapIt against writing a <code>vtkWrapJulia</code> generator from scratch. Verdict: <strong>WrapIt + permanent companion layer</strong>. It already solves AST traversal, <code>&amp;</code>/<code>*</code> overload pairs, inheritance mapping, and CxxWrap emission — for the CFD subset it produced 63 classes / 1253 methods / 9.3k LOC of compiling C++ in ~1 s. The pre-spike fear that <code>vtkSmartPointer</code> would sink automation turned out moot: VTK&#39;s public API returns raw pointers, which WrapIt maps to non-owning <code>CxxPtr</code>.</p><h2 id="The-companion-layer" tabindex="-1">The companion layer <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-companion-layer" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The companion layer {#The-companion-layer}&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">piece</th><th style="text-align:right;">why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>wrapit.toml.template</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">48 input headers — <strong>every link of every inheritance chain</strong> must be listed or Julia-side upcasts break (<code>MethodError</code> on inherited calls)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>veto.txt</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">workaround for upstream Bug 1: WrapIt silently truncates output (exit 0!) on macro-generated methods (<code>vtkSetMacro</code>, …); the driver auto-vetoes until generation completes</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">post-processor in <code>build.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">workaround for Bug 2: AOS leaf arrays&#39; real parent is a template, so their Julia base is re-pointed at <code>vtkDataArray</code> with <code>jlcxx::SuperType</code> (the same fake-superclass trick as the hand wrapper)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>shim.cpp</code> (~25 LOC)</td><td style="text-align:right;">accessors WrapIt cannot emit: string/boolean macros (<code>SetName</code>, <code>SetFileName</code>, <code>SetInsideOut</code>, …)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>conformance.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">CI gate: unit-hex → clip (tetrahedron oracle) → write/read <code>.vtu</code>, all through generated bindings</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Ownership of factory/getter returns stays in the hand-written layer (<code>take_ref</code> + smart-pointer traits) regardless of generator — the two concerns are orthogonal by design.</p><h2 id="Extending-the-wrapped-surface" tabindex="-1">Extending the wrapped surface <a class="header-anchor" href="#Extending-the-wrapped-surface" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Extending the wrapped surface {#Extending-the-wrapped-surface}&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><p>Add the class&#39;s header — and any missing intermediate base-class headers — to <code>input</code> in <code>wrapit.toml.template</code>.</p></li><li><p>Run <code>build.jl</code>; new macro-generated accessors are auto-vetoed (watch for &quot;vetoed (WrapIt bug 1)&quot; lines).</p></li><li><p>If an accessor you need got vetoed, add it to <code>shim.cpp</code>.</p></li><li><p>Verify with <code>conformance.jl</code>, then with the main test suite.</p></li></ol><h2 id="Upstream-bugs-report-candidates" tabindex="-1">Upstream bugs (report candidates) <a class="header-anchor" href="#Upstream-bugs-report-candidates" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Upstream bugs (report candidates) {#Upstream-bugs-report-candidates}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Documented with repros in <a href="https://github.com/ffrancesco94/VTK.jl/blob/main/docs/wrapit-spike.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>docs/wrapit-spike.md</code></a>: (1) silent truncation on macro-generated methods — the one that matters; (2) <code>inheritances</code> ignored for template parents; (3) Julia keywords exported; (4) <code>std::basic_ostream</code> wrapped into uncompilable code.</p></div>`,3)])])}const m=t(s,[["render",o]]);export{g as __pageData,m as default};
